**Candidate selection** is the decision to move one compound out of the lab and toward human testing. It is a big moment because the next steps cost a lot of money and time.

Before choosing, the team checks that the compound:
- works strongly and selectively on its target;
- works in cells and in animal models of the disease;
- has good pharmacokinetics: it is absorbed, lasts long enough and is cleared in a predictable way;
- shows a good **safety margin**, meaning the amount that causes problems in animals is much higher than the amount expected to work;
- passes early safety screens, like hERG (heart rhythm) and the Ames test (DNA damage);
- can be made on a large scale, with a stable solid form.

The team compares this against the **target product profile**, the description of what the final medicine must do.

The chosen compound becomes the **preclinical candidate**. It then goes through formal safety (toxicology) studies, usually in two animal species. If those go well, the company can apply to regulators to start clinical trials in humans. Teams usually also pick **backup compounds** from the same or a different series, in case the first one fails.
